        {
          "q": "This question has three parts. A furniture workshop makes tables and chairs. Each table requires 4 hours of carpentry and 2 hours of finishing; each chair requires 1 hour of carpentry and 1 hour of finishing. Each week the shop has at most 32 hours of carpentry time and 20 hours of finishing time available. Each table earns a profit of 70 dollars and each chair earns a profit of 30 dollars. Let $x$ be the number of tables and $y$ the number of chairs produced in a week. (a) Write the system of inequalities describing the constraints (including $x\\geq0$ and $y\\geq0$). (b) Find the vertices of the feasible region. (c) Determine the number of tables and chairs that maximizes the weekly profit $P=70x+30y$, and find the maximum profit.",
          "answer": "(a) The constraints are $x\\geq0$, $y\\geq0$, $4x+y\\leq32$ (carpentry), and $2x+y\\leq20$ (finishing). (b) The boundary lines meet the axes and each other at $(0,0)$, $(8,0)$, $(6,8)$, and $(0,20)$ \u2014 these are the vertices of the feasible region. (c) Evaluating $P=70x+30y$: $(0,0)\\to0$; $(8,0)\\to560$; $(6,8)\\to660$; $(0,20)\\to600$. The maximum profit is $660$ dollars, achieved by producing $6$ tables and $8$ chairs per week."
